react-native-enriched-markdown 1.1.1 (Android): a spacer line was measured 46 px
short, so a message plate ended a fraction of a line above its own last line.

MarginBottomSpan turns a lone "\n" into a spacer exactly marginBottom tall by
overwriting the line's FontMetrics; LineHeightSpan then grows any line with
spare leading up to lineHeight. Both edit `fm` in place, so the result depends
on which runs last. Android does not call them in the same order for the
shadow-node StaticLayout (MeasurementStore.measureAndCache*, text is the raw
SpannableStringBuilder: LineHeightSpan, then MarginBottomSpan -> 10 px) and for
the TextView's layout (the text is the NoCopy buffer wrapped by the emoji
transformation method: MarginBottomSpan, then LineHeightSpan -> 56 px). Yoga
therefore sizes the view from 10 px spacers while the TextView draws 56 px ones,
and the last line overflows the view. Every spacer (blank line before a code
block, heading margin) is affected; a long reply adds up to about one line.

Fix: LineHeightSpan leaves spacer lines alone, so the order stops mattering and
measured height equals drawn height. Italic and custom fonts were never the
cause: measurement does use the spans' typeface (TextSpan.updateMeasureState).
iOS measures the attributed string it draws, so it needs no change.

Remove this patch when upstream makes the two spans order-independent.

Android container lifecycle: shutting down the render executor does not cancel
results already queued on the main Handler, nor work which ignores interruption
and posts after shutdown. A dropped EnrichedMarkdown could still apply segments,
request image remeasurement through a stale Fabric StateWrapper, or submit work
to its shutdown executor. Mark the final view drop as disposed, invalidate render
ids, clear queued callbacks and the state wrapper, and ignore subsequent render,
prop-commit and image-layout work. Do not gate on window attachment: legitimate
rendering before first attachment and temporary detachment must still work.

Remove the lifecycle hunks and regression tests once upstream protects the
container's permanent teardown against these late callbacks. This is an
independently verified native lifecycle defect, not proof that it caused a
particular user-reported file-preview close crash.

diff --git a/android/src/main/java/com/swmansion/enriched/markdown/spans/LineHeightSpan.kt b/android/src/main/java/com/swmansion/enriched/markdown/spans/LineHeightSpan.kt
--- a/android/src/main/java/com/swmansion/enriched/markdown/spans/LineHeightSpan.kt
+++ b/android/src/main/java/com/swmansion/enriched/markdown/spans/LineHeightSpan.kt
@@ -30,6 +30,18 @@ class LineHeightSpan(
     fm: Paint.FontMetricsInt?,
   ) {
     if (fm == null) return
+    // A spacer line (a lone "\n" carrying a MarginBottomSpan) is exactly
+    // marginBottom tall, whatever the line height. Both spans edit `fm` in
+    // place, so the result depends on which runs last, and Android does not
+    // call them in the same order for the shadow-node StaticLayout and for
+    // the TextView's own layout (it differs once the TextView wraps its text
+    // in a transformation method / selectable buffer). Leave spacers alone so
+    // the order stops mattering and measured height == drawn height.
+    if (text is android.text.Spanned && end - start == 1 && text[start] == '\n' &&
+      text.getSpans(start, end, MarginBottomSpan::class.java).isNotEmpty()
+    ) {
+      return
+    }
 
     val leading = lineHeight - ((-fm.ascent) + fm.descent)
     if (leading <= 0) return

